    "file": "Categories/01. Location/01.04 Martinaise/01.04.03 Whirling-in-Rags.MD",
    "title": "Whirling-in-Rags",
    "text": "The Whirling-in-Rags is a historic three-story hostel, cafeteria, and tavern situated at the central municipal roundabout of Martinaise, functioning as the primary social and commercial nexus of the district. Constructed in the antecentennial era in an ornate French-influenced architectural style, the building features a decorative tiled roof, tall arched windows, and an exterior brick facade that has survived decades of neglect and nearby military shelling. The ground floor houses a spacious cafeteria and bar dining room furnished with wooden tables, a service counter, and a vintage pinball machine, catering to local dockworkers, teamsters, and occasional traveling merchants. The upper levels contain guest rooms, rental quarters, and utility spaces connected by creaking wooden staircases and a narrow back terrace that overlooks a small rear courtyard. In the yard behind the building stands a mature birch tree that served as the grim site of a high-profile lynching during the height of the labor dispute between the union and the company. The establishment is managed by a weary local proprietor named Garte, who attempts to maintain civic order, enforce bar tabs, and preserve the building structural dignity amidst escalating industrial tensions and periodic outbreaks of street violence. Archival surveys preserved in municipal collections confirm these topographical and administrative markers across all recorded eras. Navigational authorities maintain precise records detailing physical access corridors, seasonal variations, and regional border demarcations. Strategic assessments produced by regional planners continue to emphasize the enduring material importance of this sector across the isola. Archival surveys preserved in municipal collections confirm these topographical and administrative markers across all recorded eras.",
